# Rust security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to Rust-specific
risks (OWASP) and the safe standard-library/ecosystem API. This file stays
broad rather than narrowing to a subdirectory, since `unsafe` and
panic-on-untrusted-input risks appear wherever a crate touches
attacker-influenced data.

## `unsafe` blocks

- Every `unsafe` block carries a `# Safety` doc comment stating the specific
  invariants (pointer validity, alignment, lifetime, no concurrent mutation)
  that make it sound.
- Keep an `unsafe` block as small as possible: wrap only the operation that
  requires it (a raw pointer deref, an FFI call, `mem::transmute`).
- Never widen an `unsafe` block or drop its `# Safety` comment to silence the
  compiler or clippy.

## Panics on untrusted input

- No `.unwrap()`/`.expect()` on a `Result`/`Option` derived from network
  input, file contents, CLI arguments, or any attacker-influenced source;
  propagate with `?` or handle explicitly.
- `.get(i)` instead of `arr[i]` for untrusted-derived indices;
  `checked_add`/`checked_mul`/`saturating_*`/`wrapping_*` as the semantics
  require instead of bare `+`/`*` on untrusted values.

## FFI boundaries

- Validate every value crossing an `extern "C"` boundary (null, validity,
  alignment before dereferencing); document the ownership/lifetime contract
  (who frees it, with which allocator) in the `# Safety` comment.
- Never let a panic unwind across an `extern "C"` boundary; catch it with
  `std::panic::catch_unwind` at the FFI edge and return an error code.

## Dependency and supply-chain hygiene

- Run `cargo audit` (or the project's equivalent) when introducing or
  updating a dependency, or when asked to check for known vulnerabilities.
- Pin dependency versions deliberately in `Cargo.toml`; no bare `*` or overly
  wide range.

## Deserialization

- When deserializing untrusted input with `serde`, apply size/depth limits
  appropriate to the format (bounded reader, recursion-depth guard, the
  deserializer's own limit configuration).
- Do not trust a `Deserialize`d type's field values as pre-validated;
  validate ranges/formats/invariants afterwards.

## Randomness and secrets

- A CSPRNG (`rand`'s `ThreadRng`/`OsRng`, `getrandom`, `ring`) for tokens,
  session IDs, keys, nonces.
- No hard-coded credential, API key, or signing secret; load from the
  environment/secret storage the project already uses.
